\section{Step 1 - Define the panel}{

Use \code{\link[=define_biomarker]{define_biomarker()}} to specify a measured column, physiological system,
unit, risk direction and optional log transform. Combine biomarkers with
\code{\link[=define_protocol]{define_protocol()}}, which fixes the method (\code{quantile_count},
\code{cutoff_count} or \code{z_mean}), the balance (each biomarker or each system
weighs equally), the missing-data policy and optional reference \code{strata}
(for example sex) and \code{age}. HDL normally uses \code{direction = "low"}; keep
its measured values unchanged. Audit inputs with \code{\link[=check_data_quality]{check_data_quality()}}.
}

\section{Step 2 - Fit a reference and calculate scores}{

\code{\link[=fit_reference]{fit_reference()}} estimates fixed thresholds, or reference means and SDs,
pooled, within strata, or as smooth functions of age (growth-chart style
centiles). Inspect them with \code{\link[=reference_cutoffs]{reference_cutoffs()}} and \code{\link[=plot_centiles]{plot_centiles()}}.
Reuse the same reference with \code{\link[=score_load]{score_load()}} to compare cohorts or visits.
\code{\link[=allostatic_load]{allostatic_load()}} combines fitting and scoring when supplied a protocol,
or scores against a supplied fitted reference.
}

\section{Step 3 - Inspect results}{

\code{as.data.frame(result)} returns participant scores (use
\code{what = "components"} or \code{what = "systems"} for biomarker contributions and
system scores), and \code{result$audit} holds calculation metadata.
\code{\link[=simulate_biomarkers]{simulate_biomarkers()}} supplies synthetic data, optionally with age and sex.
}

\section{Step 4 - Choose and customize a graphic}{

\code{\link[=plot_data]{plot_data()}} returns data for your own \code{ggplot2::ggplot()} code. \code{\link[=plot_load]{plot_load()}}
(or \code{plot(result)}) selects an optional preset; \code{allostatic_load(..., graphics = "auto")}
builds the full preset collection (including cutoff panels via
\code{plot_load(result, "cutoffs")}). \code{\link[=plot_centiles]{plot_centiles()}} shows age-specific curves
and \code{\link[=plot_risk_map]{plot_risk_map()}} individual contributions. \code{\link[=theme_allostatic]{theme_allostatic()}} provides
an optional theme.
}

\section{Step 5 - Explain differences and patterns}{

\code{\link[=decompose_gap]{decompose_gap()}} splits a difference in mean load between two groups
exactly into biomarker or system contributions. \code{\link[=risk_patterns]{risk_patterns()}} shows
which combinations of biomarkers make up each load, their co-occurrence and
the redundancy of the panel.
}

\section{Step 6 - Quantify uncertainty and robustness}{

\code{\link[=measurement_uncertainty]{measurement_uncertainty()}} propagates analytical and within-person
biological variation to each score; \code{\link[=visit_changes]{visit_changes()}} can classify changes
with reference change values. \code{\link[=bootstrap_load]{bootstrap_load()}} measures
reference-calibration uncertainty. \code{\link[=sensitivity_analysis]{sensitivity_analysis()}},
\code{\link[=panel_influence]{panel_influence()}}, \code{\link[=compare_scores]{compare_scores()}} and the multiverse tools
\code{\link[=multiverse_grid]{multiverse_grid()}} and \code{\link[=multiverse_analysis]{multiverse_analysis()}} assess scoring choices.
}

\section{Step 7 - Handle missing data explicitly}{

Complete panels are the default. \code{\link[=check_data_quality]{check_data_quality()}} audits inputs and
\code{\link[=convert_units]{convert_units()}} applies an explicit factor and offset.
\code{\link[=impute_biomarkers]{impute_biomarkers()}} performs explicit multiple imputation (random
